{
  "title": "Shrink images and videos before uploading",
  "tagline": "A 10 MB photo becomes 200 KB; a 30 MB video a third of that.",
  "icon": "🗜️",
  "topic": "react-native",
  "tags": [
    "React Native",
    "Android",
    "iOS"
  ],
  "readingTime": "9 min",
  "effectiveDate": "2026-09-28",
  "lastUpdated": "2026-09-28",
  "intro": [
    "Phones shoot files far larger than any server needs. Compressing on the device first makes uploads faster, cheaper on mobile data and kinder to the backend."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "summary": "The same path for every file; only the compressor differs.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "flow",
          "caption": "Pick, copy, compress, upload, clean up.",
          "stages": [
            {
              "items": [
                {
                  "label": "Pick the file",
                  "detail": "The document picker returns a uri; iOS needs the file:// prefix stripped first",
                  "explain": [
                    "react-native-document-picker hands back a DocumentPickerResponse with uri, name, type and size.",
                    "Android gives a content:// uri that must be used as is; iOS gives file:///…, and the file APIs want the plain path without the scheme."
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "items": [
                {
                  "label": "Copy to cache",
                  "detail": "rn-fetch-blob copies the file into the app cache, where the app may read and write it freely",
                  "explain": [
                    "The picked file often lives in another app's storage; a copy in CacheDir is yours, so the compressor can open it without permission errors.",
                    "CacheDir is also a place the OS may clear on its own, which is exactly right for a file you only need until the upload ends."
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "items": [
                {
                  "label": "Image",
                  "detail": "react-native-compressor resizes and re-encodes on both platforms in one call",
                  "explain": [
                    "Image.compress(path, { maxWidth: 1000, maxHeight: 1000, quality: 0.5 }) caps the long side at 1000 px and halves the JPEG quality.",
                    "Tested with jpg, jpeg and png. A 9,388,730-byte photo came out at 222,208 bytes on iOS; a 10,506,316-byte one at 140,231 on Android."
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "label": "Video",
                  "detail": "iOS compresses while picking; Android needs a small native module around Mp4Composer",
                  "explain": [
                    "On iOS, react-native-image-crop-picker takes compressVideoPreset and hands back an already compressed file.",
                    "The picker cannot compress video on Android, so a Java module re-encodes it with Mp4Composer at a chosen size and bitrate.",
                    "Bitrate is file size ÷ duration × 8, scaled by quality: a 30 MB, 60 s clip at quality 0.5 is re-encoded at about 2 Mbit/s."
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "items": [
                {
                  "label": "Upload, then delete",
                  "detail": "Upload the compressed copy, then unlink it so the cache does not grow with every file",
                  "explain": [
                    "Check exists before unlink: a retry or a second tap can try to delete a file that is already gone.",
                    "Delete in a finally block, so a failed upload does not leave a 20 MB video behind in the cache."
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "columns": [
            "Library",
            "Used for"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "react-native-document-picker 8.1.0",
